      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Denis's approach to colonial themes diverges sharply from Hollywood's typical treatment, seen in films like **The African Queen** (1951) or <a href=\"/movie/606\" class=\"movie-title\">Out of Africa</a> (1985). Where those films often romanticize the colonial experience, **White Material** presents a brutal, immediate portrait of a world coming violently undone. The film's visual style, crafted by cinematographer Yves Cape, creates a suffocating atmosphere through harsh lighting and unstable handheld shots that echo similar techniques in **The Battle of Algiers** (1966)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's treatment of violence and political upheaval shares DNA with other African-set political thrillers like **The Constant Gardener** (2005) and **Blood Diamond** (2006), but Denis strips away any pretense of white savior narratives. Instead, Maria's stubborn refusal to acknowledge reality becomes a metaphor for colonial blindness itself, creating a tense character study that builds to an inevitable breakdown. SUBHEAD: Colonial Legacy and Modern Africa"
      },
